r homes or  potentially needing to find new homes  and many of them very frustrated by the  length of time that it's taking to carry  out these investigations  the london fire brigade haven't given  any indication on how long this  investigation might take but they have  said that their workers are trying to  make sure that the area is safe that  they need to assess the extent of the  damage and crucially to find out what  the cause of the fire was  well let's look at these the highest  temperatures ever in three of the four  nations of the uk  the 40.3 degree mark degree marker  didn't just break the uk record but it  obliterated it in the words of one  climate scientist it's so far from the  norm if a record was broken by this much  in sport well foul play would be  suspected  and this is the guilty party man-made  climate change here is the graph of  average global temperature rises since  1880 when the industrial revolution  took off  when it's hotter on average the spikes  are likely to be higher too  well the flames reached wellington  because they spread across fields and  across britain hundreds of acres have  gone up in flames this is a threat to  food supplies farming comes and wildlife  well let's talk to a farmer who has felt  this firsthand  tom martin runs a family farm near  peterborough and cambridgeshire and his  whole fields were nearly caught up in  tuesday's fires tom you were there  yourself what actually happened  well that's right we could see our  neighbour's farm from our farm and we  could see whether um uh that the  telegraph wire had uh had struck against  a tree uh which of course the wire had  sagged in the in the heat uh it had  struck the ground uh and uh set light to  to the hedgerow and about 100 yards of  the hedgerow were burning and  threatening the crops in the field next  door  and really what was at stake here if if  this had taken off and the fire actually  got beyond the hedge  well this was at two o'clock on tuesday  afternoon when the temperature was 38  degrees celsius and the winds were 20  miles an hour and there are all around  me hundreds of acres of standing grain  uh worth about two and a half thousand  pounds per hectare uh so there's a huge  amount of value but of course it's not  just that it's a potential loss a threat  of life as well  you've farmed in that area for quite a  while and presumably some of your fields  border villages as well are you thinking  this threat of fire might well change  the way you sort of plant and grow crops  yeah we've always been uh very wary  about fire um but this things are really  changing now it is is extreme to levels  that we've not experienced before um we  probably won't be changing the the types  of crops but we'll be looking at more  drought resistant varieties and we'll be  trying to improve our soil health so  that it retains the moisture better and  allows our plants to stay greener longer  well i wanted to talk to you about  drought because that's perhaps the  somewhat forgotten story of this year  because it doesn't come in a in a  massive peak like we saw with the heat  but just how dry is it in in your part  of eastern england  it's incredibly dry our rainfall is down  about 40 we haven't had meaningful rain  here for probably two to three months  and the ground around me is cracked uh  the clay soils have really shrunk up uh  and and it's desperately desperately dry  it's a tinderbox  yeah and what do you think that will  mean in terms of your future in terms of  maybe the varieties of crop you grow or  anything are there things you can plant  that would be more drought tolerant  well as we make decisions as farmers  really from now forward uh all our  decisions will have to be referencing  the climate we've seen more extremes of  hot and cold of of wet and dry and so  we're gonna have to be thinking uh in  all our decisions about about climate uh  and the extremes that we've seen  well thanks very much indeed tom i hope  some of those great clouds behind your  head might actually bear some rain for  you but i have my doubts thanks very  much indeed  well alongside the destructive heat wave  the other big story this week has been  the contest to be the next leader of the  conservative party and prime minister  well it's fair to say that none of the  candidates have really been championing  their own green credentials yesterday  president of cop 26 that's last year's  big climate summit in glasgow alok  sharma called on the next prime minister  to make the net zero target a key focus  what i would certainly want to see from  any future prime minister is a  laser-like focus on ensuring that we are  delivering on our policies on net zero  missions but at the same time  pushing forward on more jobs more growth  more inward investment which we have  seen coming in the last few days uh i  have been a real wake-up call for  everyone in this country it's what very  very many millions of people across the  world are experiencing on a regular  basis we have to deal with this issue  so let's have a look at the two  remaining contenders  rishi sunak has committed to reaching  net zero emissions by  2050 and he was part of the government  that was committed to this target too  but he has expressed some caution saying  the move to net zero should not be too  hard and fast and we should prioritize  jobs as chancellor he cut fuel duty and  tax on short-haul domestic flights but  also introduced a 5 billion pound tax on  oil and gas companies  he's pledged to limit building any new  onshore wind farms and wants the focus  to be offshore instead  this would be part of a policy to make  the uk energy self-sufficient by 2045.  liz truss has also committed to net zero  but in a similar way to sunac she said  we need to find better ways to deliver  it that won't harm people and businesses  and just this morning she pledged a  moratorium that's in effect a suspension  on so-called green levees to help cut  