 emergency response but of course  these nations are looking forward now  knowing that climate change is going to  make fire season worse and heat worse  and they're really starting to think  about how they can live with this new  reality well do they have any ideas  about how that might happen and could  there be lessons for us here in the uk  well these countries are obviously hot  but now they're getting hotter they  understand how to arrange their day to  avoid the hottest part of the day in the  summer months but there are recent  adaptations for example in seville  they've appointed a chief heat officer  and they've started naming heat waves as  if you might name a hurricane so people  can get to the grips with the idea that  these things are life-threatening events  and then you've perhaps got the most  extreme adaptation of all and in both  portugal and in spain people told me  that they were genuinely thinking about  whether or not they could stay put in  their communities if the threat from  climate change keeps growing  wow sort of depopulation of the country  so quite extraordinary hannah thomas  peter thank you very much indeed  in america 100 million u.s citizens are  under heat warnings this week with  little let up in sight and yesterday  president biden vowed to combat the  climate crisis  climate change is literally an  existential threat to our nation and to  the world so my message today is this  since congress is not acting as it  should and these guys here are but we're  not getting many republican votes this  is an emergency an emergency and i will  i will look at it that way i said last  week uh say it again loud and clear as  president i'll use my executive powers  to combat climate the climate crisis and  the absence of congressional action  well joining me from the u.s is rachel  kite an expert in climate change and  policy from tufts university in  massachusetts and a member of the un  secretary general's climate advisory  group rachel very very good to see you  the big question for me here is when you  combine this extraordinary weather  across much of the northern hemisphere  with the pressure on fuel price rises  are we finally seeing a bit of a policy  change towards perhaps more  climate-friendly ideas  well we need to um but i think that in  the very good session you've just  shown with the  your commentators and the filming we're  still and too many political leaders are  sort of thinking about the economy on  the one side and then climate and its  impacts on the other  in fact the answers lie in economic  policy the future of our ability to  combat climate change and live with it  depends on how we integrate our it into  our economic planning so  in the uk um  we we have to uh change the way that we  generate energy we're going to have to  invest a lot in communities to make them  more resilient  and if we don't we're going to be in  trouble it's also  a case that 80 of the world now has a  net zero target so it's really about  implementing change uh not seeing that  climate change is something divorced  from the economy the future of the  economy and its competitiveness depends  on the way in which we will combat  climate change rachel sort of below if  you like high level politics what are we  seeing in terms of  investment decisions and actual build  out of renewable energy resources for  instance what's the trend there  well i think that the trend is that  renewable energy has been uh growing  exponentially uh around the world it  started to slow down a little bit during  the sort of pandemic years but now we  need to pick it up again and of course  we now are pivoting away from russian  hydrocarbons in europe etcetera so we  need to continue this exponential growth  of installation of renewable energy but  we need to do it everywhere and i think  the the point is that we're just not  going fast enough and so it really does  matter that we incentivize renewable  energy installation and that's where u.s  congress is having problems and it's  where candidates for the uk premiership  are also having problems  so we do need to expand it and then we  need to invest in how to make  communities cope with climate change  better  and just from your u.s experience um  just briefly if you can what kind of  pressures is biden under uh in the sort  of the federal level but also what's  happening on the state level if you  could compress those two for me  well at the state level um mayors and  cities are doing what they can and here  in boston  the mayor mayor wu has a 26 point  resilience plan to help the city become  a cooler city because we're going to get  the kinds of heat waves we've got this  week on a continued basis  the problem is at the federal level  politics has meant that climate change  has become a pawn and while there is not  so much climate denial there is a lot of  climate delay and that delay is as  dangerous as denial when we've got this  kind of weather rachel kite i'm terribly  sorry we will have to leave it there but  thank you very much indeed for your time  well that is all from us today i'll be  back at the weekend with a special  program on aviation asking if we have a  hope of keeping our flight connected  world without cooking the planet well  chuck's away and back to you sarah jane  tom thank you i just want to update  viewers on the breaking news we brought  you just before the climate show the  special edition that is president biden  has tested positive for covid we've just  heard from the president's doctor who  said that president biden felt tired had  a runny nose and a dry cough  typical symptoms of kovid he said that  potus went to bed and felt fine but  didn't sleep well and tested positive  this morning a spokesperson for boris  johnson has said that  boris johnson has sent president biden  his best wishes and hopes he has a  speedy recovery from covert more after  the break  [Music]  you

